length:var(--msg-code-block-size,0.875rem)] !leading-relaxed p-3.5" style="color: rgb(234, 236, 240); background: transparent; font-family: var(--font-mono);"><code class="language-sql" data-highlight="final" style="color: rgb(234, 236, 240); background: transparent; font-family: var(--font-mono); white-space: pre;"><span class="block [unicode-bidi:inherit] [text-align:-webkit-match-parent] [text-align:match-parent]" data-code-line-group=""><span><span><span style="color: rgb(204, 123, 244);">select</span>
</span><span>    order_id,
</span><span>    order_item_id,
</span><span>    product_id,
</span><span>    seller_id,
</span><span><span style="color: rgb(112, 184, 255);">    cast</span>(shipping_limit_date <span style="color: rgb(204, 123, 244);">as timestamp</span>) <span style="color: rgb(204, 123, 244);">as</span> shipping_limit_at,
</span><span><span style="color: rgb(112, 184, 255);">    cast</span>(price <span style="color: rgb(204, 123, 244);">as</span> double)         <span style="color: rgb(204, 123, 244);">as</span> price,
</span><span><span style="color: rgb(112, 184, 255);">    cast</span>(freight_value <span style="color: rgb(204, 123, 244);">as</span> double) <span style="color: rgb(204, 123, 244);">as</span> freight_value
</span><span><span style="color: rgb(204, 123, 244);">from</span> {{ source(<span style="color: rgb(155, 233, 99);">'raw'</span>, <span style="color: rgb(155, 233, 99);">'order_items'</span>) }}</span></span></span></code></pre></div></div></div>
<p dir="ltr">Now write the other six the same way, selecting only the columns you need:</p>
<div dir="ltr" class="md-table-scroll prose-scroll print:overflow-x-visible touch:scroll-fade-x focus-visible:mask-none [:root:not([dir=rtl])_&amp;:dir(rtl)]:mask-none"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Model</th><th scope="col">Source table</th><th scope="col">Keep these columns</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code dir="ltr">stg_customers</code></td><td>customers</td><td>customer_id, customer_unique_id, customer_zip_code_prefix, customer_city, customer_state</td></tr><tr><td><code dir="ltr">stg_payments</code></td><td>order_payments</td><td>order_id, payment_sequential, payment_type, payment_installments, payment_value</td></tr><tr><td><code dir="ltr">stg_reviews</code></td><td>order_reviews</td><td>review_id, order_id, review_score, review_creation_date, review_answer_timestamp</td></tr><tr><td><code dir="ltr">stg_products</code></td><td>products</td><td>product_id, product_category_name, product_weight_g, product_length_cm, product_height_cm, product_width_cm</td></tr><tr><td><code dir="ltr">stg_sellers</code></td><td>sellers</td><td>seller_id, seller_zip_code_prefix, seller_city, seller_state</td></tr><tr><td><code dir="ltr">stg_category_translation</code></td><td>product_category_name_translation</td><td>product_category_name, product_category_name_english</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p dir="ltr">Skip geolocation for the MVP. It has about a million rows with many duplicates per zip code, and none of your marts need it.</p>
<p dir="ltr"><strong>Important concept:</strong> <code dir="ltr">customer_id</code> is generated <em>per order</em>. <code dir="ltr">customer_unique_id</code> identifies the actual person. Repeat-purchase analysis must use <code dir="ltr">customer_unique_id</code>.</p>
<ol dir="ltr" start="4">
<li>Create <code dir="ltr">olist/models/staging/_staging.yml</code> with tests on the keys:</li>
</ol>
